From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:11:41 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 34/60] hjviewer: same-account login: headless commands for the
 account E2E
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The account E2E (design v3 7-4; tools/e2e/firewall-e2e.sh of the hjviewer repository) runs real
clients against hjv-api, so the test feature hjv-e2e gets the commands for it. They send what the
app sends, through ui_interface::http_request, where the account hooks seal the password, sign
the requests and observe the answers; a login on an installed PC is all it takes to bind it, as
in the app.

  --hjv-e2e-login <portal ID> <password>   the login dialog's request, then this-device
  --hjv-e2e-logout [--during-devices]      [로그아웃]; with the flag, a device list request is
                                           still out when the logout's hook runs
  --hjv-e2e-devices, --hjv-e2e-keys        the device tab's list, the logged-in devices
  --hjv-e2e-remove <PC ID>, --hjv-e2e-revoke-key <ckid>
  --hjv-e2e-option <name> [<value> ...]    the service's options over its IPC; several pairs go
                                           in one write, as the logout hook writes its unbind
  --hjv-e2e-local-option <name> [<value>]  this user's LocalConfig
  --hjv-e2e-service-password <password>    the service's permanent password, over its IPC
  --hjv-e2e-id                             this device's ID and public key
  --hjv-e2e-cm                             a connection manager that prints the peer and the
                                           name the service shows for each login

A session command (--hjv-e2e-screenshot, -stream, -forward) with an empty password is a
password-less connection. It now ends with exit code 3 when the session asks for the password
("hjv-e2e: password required <title>"), with 4 on an 'error' box ("hjv-e2e: error <title>"), and
the two desktop commands with 1 when no screen arrived. The commands also set the app's log up
(name hjv-e2e), which they used to return before.

One seam outside the test file, compiled with the feature only: HJV_E2E_KEY_LOCKED makes the
Linux key store say that the account key cannot be read now, as a locked iPhone's Keychain does,
so that the E2E sees such a request stay unsent ("hjv: account key unavailable") and nobody
logged out.

A build without the feature is unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
